==============================================================================================================================
1.  INDEPENDENT RE-DERIVATION OF THE HEADLINE NUMBER
==============================================================================================================================
  [PASS] V0 my own Jeans solver reproduces sigma^4 = (4/81) G M a_0 (Milgrom 1994), so any disagreement with g06 below is a disagreement about the data or the prescription and not about my integrator   (sigma^4/(G M a_0) = 0.049388 vs 4/81 = 0.049383)
  UNGC re-read independently: 869 galaxies -> 26 groups, 333 members, N per group 5-39
  independent median boost: canonical 0.817 (g06 quotes 0.817), alt 0.746 (g06 quotes 0.746); bootstrap 16-84% [0.656, 1.110] (g06 (0.656, 1.11))
  median g_bar/a_0 = 0.00189; per-group boosts span 0.23 - 6.01
  [PASS] V1 the headline reproduces under an independent re-implementation of the whole chain (catalogue read, membership, gapper, baryon budget, baryonic external field, Jeans solve).  If this failed the claim would be dead on arithmetic; it is not, and everything below is about the INFERENCE   (canonical 0.8172 vs 0.817 (+0.0001 dex); alt 0.7455 vs 0.746 (-0.0003 dex))

==============================================================================================================================
2.  ATTACK 1 -- IS THE MEDIAN A PROPERTY OF THE SAMPLE, OR OF ITS WORST-MEASURED HALF?
==============================================================================================================================
  boost = (sigma_obs/sigma_pred)^2, and in the deep-MOND limit of a point mass 3 sigma_pred^2 = sqrt(G M a_0)
  is nearly radius-independent (g06's own check J3).  So the boost is, to within the transition correction,
          boost  ~  3 sigma_obs^2 / sqrt(G M_b a_0),
  i.e. a baryonic-Tully-Fisher-style test in which ALL the per-group scatter enters through sigma_obs.  A
  5-member gapper dispersion is the noisiest and the most membership-sensitive quantity in the file, and the
  sample runs from 5 to 39 members.  So: does the boost run with membership number?
    variable                      Spearman rho   permutation p
    members per group N                 +0.330          0.1026
    host distance D                     +0.071          0.7314
    log baryonic mass                   +0.079          0.6994
  split at N = 10: rich half N_grp = 9, median boost 1.431 (+0.156 dex); poor half N_grp = 17, median boost 0.604 (-0.219 dex); gap +0.375 dex
  the same split on 20000 label-permuted samples: |gap| >= 0.375 dex in 8.11% of them
  [PASS] A1 the boost does NOT run with the number of members, i.e. the headline is not being set by the sparsest and least reliable groups.  This is the data-quality confound: sigma_obs from 5 velocities is both the noisiest and the most membership-sensitive number in the chain, and if the boost tracks N then the median over 26 groups is a statement about catalogue depth rather than about gravity   (Spearman rho(N, log boost) = +0.330, permutation p = 0.1026; rich (N>=10) median 1.431 vs poor (N<10) median 0.604, a gap of +0.375 dex (split permutation p = 0.0811))
  [FAIL] A2 the well-measured subsample carries the claim.  Restricting to the {N>=10} groups -- the ones with enough members for the gapper to mean anything -- must leave the separation from the liability table's X-ray group/cluster median of 2.11 substantially intact (asserted here at 0.30 dex, against the claimed 0.412)   (rich-subsample median boost 1.431; separation from the cluster median +0.169 dex, against the full-sample +0.412 dex.  The liability table's mildest X-ray group/cluster rows are 1.45 and 1.48)
  [PASS] A3 MUTATION CONTROL for attack 1 -- shuffle the membership numbers across groups.  The N-trend must vanish when the labels are broken; if a shuffled N still correlated with the boost, attack 1 would be an artefact of my own ranking rather than a property of the sample   (shuffled rho = -0.019 +- 0.196; real rho = +0.330, i.e. 1.68 sigma from the shuffled null)
  
  Is an N-trend just small-sample bias in the gapper?  Monte-Carlo, 20000 draws per n: n-1 Gaussian tracers
  plus one host pinned at the systemic velocity (the host IS in the member list and sits at the centre):
       n   median gapper / true sigma   -> bias in boost (dex)
       5                       0.8490                  -0.1422
       6                       0.8758                  -0.1152
       8                       0.9107                  -0.0813
      10                       0.9320                  -0.0612
      20                       0.9684                  -0.0279
      39                       0.9833                  -0.0146
  [FAIL] A4 the rich-vs-poor gap IS explained away by the gapper's own small-sample behaviour, in which case attack 1 is a statistics artefact and the claim survives it.  This check is deliberately phrased AGAINST my own attack: PASS = my attack is an estimator artefact and I withdraw it; FAIL = the gap is bigger than the estimator can produce and the confound is real.  The n = 5 bias goes the RIGHT way to help the claim (sparse groups read low), so it had to be sized rather than waved at   (estimator bias n=5 vs n=39 predicts -0.128 dex in the boost; the observed rich-vs-poor gap is +0.375 dex, a factor 2.9 larger)

==============================================================================================================================
3.  ATTACK 2 -- THE '3.6 SIGMA' USES ONLY THE STATISTICAL ERROR.  THE FILE'S OWN SYSTEMATICS ARE LARGER.
==============================================================================================================================
    systematic (admissible branches only)             low     high  half-width dex
    stellar M/L: Upsilon_K = 0.4 vs 1.0             1.011    0.621           0.106
    hot gas: f_hot = 0 vs 1.0                       0.912    0.746           0.044
    external field: isolated vs x3                  0.706    0.909           0.055
    aperture: r_h vs 3 r_max                        0.648    0.873           0.065
  statistical half-width on the median (bootstrap)          = 0.114 dex  -> 3.61 sigma
  quadrature sum of the ADMISSIBLE prescription systematics = 0.143 dex
  stat + sys                                                = 0.183 dex  -> 2.26 sigma
  and this still counts NOTHING on the cluster side (hydrostatic bias, abundance-matched stellar masses),
  and EXCLUDES the two branches g06 itself rules out by argument: the pure external-field limit (0.208) and
  the raw 2M++ field (2.215, which lands on the cluster median).
  [FAIL] B1 the claimed separation from the cluster rows survives the file's OWN admissible systematic bracket at three sigma.  The quoted 3.6 sigma is explicitly 'on the group side's error alone'; a referee will not accept a statistical-only error bar on a comparison whose dominant uncertainty is the stellar M/L   (separation +0.412 dex; 3.61 sigma statistical-only, 2.26 sigma once the admissible systematics are included)
  [PASS] B2 the separation survives the SINGLE worst admissible systematic on its own -- the stellar M/L.  Upsilon_K = 1.0 is a defensible old-population K-band value (Bell & de Jong 2001 span roughly 0.6-1.0 in K for red colours), and this rung's whole baryon budget is 93% host K-band light   (at Upsilon_K = 1.0 the median boost is 0.621, separation from 2.11 = +0.531 dex against 3 x 0.114)

==============================================================================================================================
4.  ATTACK 3 -- IS THE PER-GROUP SCATTER CONSISTENT WITH THE QUOTED ERRORS, OR IS THERE UNMODELLED SYSTEMATIC?
==============================================================================================================================
  observed scatter of log boost   = 0.415 dex over 26 groups
  quoted statistical scatter      = 0.276 dex (rms of the per-group 2/sqrt(2(N-1)) errors)
  implied INTRINSIC/unmodelled    = 0.310 dex
  chi^2 about the median = 78.8 for 25 dof  (p = <1e-4)
  [FAIL] C1 the 26 boosts are statistically consistent with a single value, so quoting a median with a bootstrap error is a legitimate summary.  If they are not, the sample carries an unmodelled per-group systematic (membership, interlopers, non-equilibrium, projection) at least as large as the effect being claimed, and the median's error bar understates what is actually known   (chi^2/dof = 3.15; the unmodelled term is 0.310 dex per group, against a quoted statistical 0.276 dex and a claimed separation of 0.412 dex)

==============================================================================================================================
5.  ATTACK 4 -- WOULD ORDINARY COLD DARK MATTER PRODUCE THE SAME SIGNATURE?  ZERO FREE PARAMETERS.
==============================================================================================================================
  Abundance matching (Moster, Naab & White 2013, MNRAS 428, 3121, z = 0: log M1 = 11.59, N = 0.0351,
  beta = 1.376, gamma = 0.608) fixes M200 from the MEASURED stellar mass with no freedom; concentration from
  Dutton & Maccio 2014 (MNRAS 441, 3359): log c200 = 0.905 - 0.101 log(M200 h / 1e12).  The SAME tracer
  profile, the SAME aperture and the SAME Jeans solver are then used, so the two frameworks are compared
  through one pipeline.  If LambdaCDM lands on these dispersions as well as the framework does, this rung
  cannot separate them and the 'counted vs modelled baryons' reading of the group/cluster contrast is one
  story among several, not a measurement.
