  LambdaCDM (abundance-matched NFW, zero free parameters): median boost 0.606 (-0.218 dex), scatter 0.450 dex
  framework (Route A kernel,        zero free parameters): median boost 0.817 (-0.088 dex), scatter 0.415 dex
  [FAIL] D1 the rung DISCRIMINATES: the framework and a zero-free-parameter abundance-matched NFW halo must not land on these dispersions equally well.  A FAIL here confirms the author's own concession that this is a non-discrimination -- and it also removes the causal reading, because if CDM reproduces the group side too then 'the deficit appears where baryons are MODELLED' is an interpretation, not a result   (LambdaCDM median boost 0.606 vs framework 0.817; the two differ by -0.130 dex against 3 x the bootstrap half-width 0.342 dex.  Scatters 0.450 (LCDM) vs 0.415 (framework) dex)

==============================================================================================================================
6.  ATTACK 5 -- THE GROUP/CLUSTER CONTRAST IS DEGENERATE WITH THE MASS ESTIMATOR
==============================================================================================================================
  The liability table carries a row that matches this rung in BOTH mass and acceleration:
      | 2.63 | 0.004 | pressure | eRASS1 groups 10^12.5-13.5 at R500 |
  This rung's abundance-matched halo masses are log M200 = 11.20 - 13.24, median 12.34 -- the SAME decade -- at g_bar/a_0 = 0.0019 against that row's 0.004.
  So the contrast is not group-mass vs cluster-mass and it is not high-acceleration vs low-acceleration.
  What differs between the two rows is the ESTIMATOR: galaxy-tracer line-of-sight dispersions here, X-ray
  hydrostatic masses there.  'Counted vs modelled baryons' is ONE reading of that difference.  The others,
  which this rung cannot exclude, all push the group side DOWN rather than the cluster side up:
    (i)   radial orbit anisotropy: an isotropic Jeans solve applied to a radially anisotropic satellite
          population under-reads the mass from a line-of-sight dispersion;
    (ii)  Theta_1 > 0 membership reaches the zero-velocity surface, so a large fraction of members sit in the
          infall region where peculiar velocities are SMALL -- g06 argues this only inflates sigma; it can
          equally deflate the line-of-sight dispersion of a sample dominated by large-radius members;
    (iii) 5-39 members with no interloper rejection beyond the catalogue's own tidal index.
  [FAIL] E1 the separation is from the cluster BLOCK and not merely from its median.  The table's mildest X-ray group/cluster rows are 1.45 and 1.48; if the rung's number is not separated from THOSE at three sigma then 'groups do not show the cluster deficit' is a statement about the top of the cluster block only   (separation from the mildest X-ray row (1.45) = +0.249 dex against 3 x 0.183 = 0.548 dex)
  [FAIL] E2 the rich (N>=10) subsample is separated from the mildest X-ray group/cluster rows.  This is the strongest form of the audit: take only the groups whose dispersions are worth trusting, and ask whether they still sit below the cluster block at all   (rich-subsample median 1.431 against the table's 1.45 and 1.48 rows: +0.006 dex, against a one-sigma stat+sys of 0.183 dex)

